Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to recreate a
Python command-line compression utility with the following specification.
Build a Python 3 command-line script (intended to run under Termux on Android,
using the shebang path /data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python) that
recursively compresses files in a directory tree using multiple compression
strategies in parallel, and reports progress and results via the loguru logging
library.
Purpose and overall behavior: - Accept a target directory (or file path) and
walk it recursively to collect candidate files for compression. - Apply one or
more compression modes (bzip2-based, gzip-based, and a custom "gziper" mode that
splits data into chunks and compresses them, with a tar-style container for
multiple files).
Select the mode via command-line arguments. - Skip files whose extensions
indicate they are already compressed or archived.
Use these exclusion sets per mode: - bz2 mode excludes: .bz2, .xz, .gz, .br,
.zst, .7z, .zip, .rar - gz mode excludes: .gz, .bz2, .xz, .br, .zst, .7z, .zip,
.rar - gziper mode excludes: .gz, .zip, .bz2, .xz, .7z, .rar, .tar - Skip files
smaller than a minimum size (default 1024 bytes). - Process files concurrently
using a ProcessPoolExecutor with a configurable worker count (default 8). - Read
files in chunks (default 524288 bytes for general chunking; 262144 bytes for
gziper chunk size).
For small files at or below a memory threshold (default 32768 bytes), read whole
into memory; for larger files, use memory-mapped I/O (mmap) where appropriate. -
Compression levels: bz2 default 9, gz default 9, gziper default 9; allow user
override via arguments. - Write outputs next to the originals with appropriate
suffixes (e.g., .bz2, .gz, and a custom suffix for gziper output); do not
overwrite existing output files unless explicitly configured, and do not
re-compress already-compressed outputs. - Preserve or skip originals according
to an option (delete or keep originals); be careful to only remove originals
after successful verified write. - Provide a helper to compress raw bytes given
an algorithm name and level (bz2 via bz2.compress, gzip via gzip.compress, with
level parameters). - Use a dataclass to hold per-run configuration/results
(e.g., counts of processed, skipped, failed files, bytes in/out, elapsed time)
with a classmethod or factory that may return a new instance from parsed
arguments (the code uses typing.Self). - Provide human-readable formatting
helpers: a size formatter producing values like "512 B", "3.4 KB", "1.2 MB",
"2.0 GB", "1.1 TB" (B shows integer, others one decimal, dividing by 1024 with
units B, KB, MB, GB, TB, capping at TB); and a time formatter that renders
elapsed seconds as a timedelta string (H:MM:SS style). - At the end, log summary
statistics: files processed, skipped, failed, total input and output sizes,
compression ratio, and total elapsed time using the human-readable helpers.
Command-line interface (argparse): - Positional or required option for the input
path (directory or file). - Options for mode selection, worker count, chunk
sizes, memory threshold, minimum file size, compression levels, keep/delete
originals, overwrite behavior, and verbosity/logging level. - Provide sensible
defaults as listed above and document them in help text.
Dependencies: - Standard library only except loguru (for logging).
Use: argparse, bz2, contextlib, gzip, mmap, sh time, concurrent.futures,
dataclasses, datetime, pathlib, typing (with `from __future__ import
annotations` for forward references). - Requires Python 3.11 or later
(typing.Self).
Error handling and edge cases: - Handle missing input path with a clear error
and non-zero exit code. - Handle permission errors, unreadable files, and broken
symlinks by logging a warning/error and counting them as failed or skipped
without aborting the whole run. - Handle empty files and files below the minimum
size by skipping them. - Handle zero-length input directories gracefully with a
summary showing zero work done. - If an output file already exists and overwrite
is not enabled, skip the file and count it as skipped. - If compression produces
output larger than the input, either keep the original (skip writing) or log a
warning, as configured; do not delete originals in that case. - Ensure partial
output files are removed if an exception occurs mid-write. - Catch exceptions
from worker processes so one failing file does not crash the pool; report it in
logs and in the failure count. - Handle KeyboardInterrupt by shutting down the
executor cleanly and logging the partial summary. - Guard the entry point with
if __name__ == "__main__" and ensure multiprocessing-safe structure (worker
functions at module top level).
Logging: - Use loguru's logger; configure a sink to stderr with a level derived
from verbosity options; log per-file results at debug/info level and
errors/warnings appropriately.
Output requirements for the reproduced script: - Produce one complete, runnable
Python script file with the shebang line above, all constants as module-level
defaults, the helper functions described, the dataclass, the worker and
compression logic, the argparse-based main, and the summary reporting. - Use
type hints throughout and `from __future__ import annotations`. - Keep the
script self-contained in a single file. ---
